Title: What They Called Recovery
Credit: Animated feature screenplay — source-developed working scene
Author: Christopher Kokkinos
Draft date: 2026-10-10
Status: Independent screenplay development under DEC-0019; refinements during production

INT. HOTEL/LIBRARY/DOCUMENT BOX - WEDNESDAY

Zoe confronts Elias about another unannounced route check. He admits it was a decision to leave. They deposit the sealed second copy in a locked document box at a print shop; both retain its retrieval code.

At the library the archive stays packed. Zoe compares N4-7 and E2-4 from her notes: broad placements, returns and ordinary closures versus narrower recoveries, transfers and holds.

ZOE
Same route doesn't mean same thing. Find something outside the drive.

She crosses out ROUTE DOES NOT EQUAL CRIME as too neat.

INT. VOSS AND VALE'S ROOM - WEDNESDAY/THURSDAY

Voss compares old paper files: genuine witness relocation, a lawful return to a grandmother, and children moved after the client changed to Meridian.

VALE
We verified the paper against the number printed on the paper.

VOSS
Not an independent check.

He calls a court through its public switchboard, gives no child's name. The supposed order has no matching record; the named judge wasn't sitting family cases there that year. Further checks rule out a simple transfer.

VALE
I remember the girl. Yellow coat.

They do not contact her mother; they don't know who is still monitored.

INT. STUDY ROOM - THURSDAY - DAY

The working copy opens without wireless access. On a whiteboard: NORMAL / OVERRIDE? / WHY.

Zoe's transition history narrows to seven rows. MONITOR to REVIEW from an external source, then REVIEW to RECOVER: OVR3 / MAN.

ZOE
A manual action. But manual doesn't prove bad.

They inspect comparison histories: ordinary returns, permanent placements and a manual transfer without override. Four override classes appear. Zoe totals 486 actions, writes NOT 486 KIDS.

Review followed by override narrows most subsequent choices to RECOVER, TRANSFER or HOLD. Ordinary reviews spread across many outcomes.

ELIAS
That's what the visible sample suggests.

A lookup supplies labels. OVR3: PROTECTIVE RECOVERY AUTHORITY. OVR4: EXECUTIVE CONTAINMENT AUTHORITY. BRG: RESTRICTED PLACEMENT BRIDGE.

ZOE
Children aren't spills.

No BRG appears in her current root. They don't call that an acquittal. A linked predecessor changes the screen: REVIEW to BRG under OVR2, then PLACE, P06-11803.

Helen and Brian's placement code.

Zoe pushes her chair back. Elias touches nothing.

ZOE
Mine started in the normal part.

She corrects her own wording: ORDINARY-LOOKING.

ZOE
My life turned on an override.

At the other table, Vale lays genuine intake information beside the false later authority.

VALE
Current information. Either someone got in or someone inside helped.

Back in the study room, Zoe erases the board, including a faint SHADOW. Outside she asks whether her original family was unsafe.

ELIAS
We can't infer that. Something was recorded. We don't know how accurate or lawful it was.

She folds separate KNOW and DON'T KNOW pages. The next question: WHO WAS ON THE OTHER SIDE?
